
After completing ALL work below, stop and hand control back to the parent loop. Do not run Shark status-transition commands yourself; report the recommended outcome in your final response so the parent can advance the workflow.

Design architecture for epic E07-F01: "Sample feature for golden test".

Check for existing architecture docs and UAT plan in epic directory. If both exist and meet exit gate criteria, advance immediately.

---

Load skills from `shark-data/skills/architecture/`:
  - `workflows/design-system.md`
  - `workflows/design-backend.md`
  - `workflows/design-database as applicable.md`

READ:
(1) Epic PRD for scope and requirements
(2) Research report for existing implementations and extension points
(3) CLAUDE.md for architecture patterns and coding standards
(4) Existing architecture docs in codebase for consistency
(5) docs/product/cross-epic-integration-map.md if present

PRODUCE two documents plus the integration maps below:

**architecture.md** — System-level design:
(1) Component overview: what changes, what stays
(2) Key technical decisions with rationale (ADRs)
(3) Data model changes (if any)
(4) Integration approach (how new code connects to existing)
(5) Migration strategy (if applicable)

**uat-plan.md** — Epic acceptance plan:
(1) UAT scenarios derived from epic PRD success criteria
(2) Acceptance test descriptions (what to verify, not how)
(3) Cross-feature integration scenarios
(4) Performance/security considerations

**E07-F01-cross-epic-map.md** — Cross-epic execution view:
REQUIRED for every new or redesigned epic. Create the file even when no
cross-epic integrations are found; in that case, record "No X-## rows
identified during design" with the current date.

Before writing it:
- Ask the architect to identify cross-epic dependencies, shared contracts,
  shared state, API boundaries, data handoffs, and sequencing risks.
- Ask the CX designer to review user journey handoffs, continuity concerns,
  state continuity, handoff copy, and product workflow implications.
- Read docs/product/cross-epic-integration-map.md if it exists. Create it from
  file_templates/cross-epic-integration-map.md when new X-## rows are needed
  and the global map does not exist.

Use this table for both the global product map and the per-epic map:

| ID | Producer epic | Consumer epic(s) | Integration purpose | Contract / shape source | UX / CX handoff notes | Owning feature | Status | Test coverage pointer |
|----|---------------|------------------|---------------------|-------------------------|-----------------------|----------------|--------|-----------------------|

Rules:
- X-## IDs are stable product-level IDs. Assign once in
  docs/product/cross-epic-integration-map.md and reference verbatim in
  E07-F01-cross-epic-map.md, decomposition, feature specs, test plans,
  producing/consuming task specs, feature review, and QA.
- X-## is only for cross-epic integrations. Keep it separate from I-##, which
  remains the per-epic cross-feature interaction ID space.
- Each X-## row names producer epic, consumer epic(s), integration purpose,
  contract / shape source, UX / CX handoff notes, owning feature once known,
  status, and test coverage pointer.
- Owning feature and test coverage pointer may be TBD at epic design. If either
  stays TBD, status remains proposed and decomposition or test planning must
  close it before their gates pass.
- Update docs/product/progress.md after changing the global map: mark the map
  present, set last updated, name E07-F01 design as the updater, and append a
  decision-log entry naming added, changed, covered, or deferred X-## rows.
- Register the per-epic document after writing it:
  `DOC_PATH="docs/plan/E07-F01/E07-F01-cross-epic-map.md" && shark related-docs add "Cross-Epic Map" "${DOC_PATH}" --epic=E07-F01`

**E07-F01-interaction-map.md** — Cross-feature wiring:
REQUIRED if this epic is expected to decompose into 3+ features. SKIP only for
single-feature epics or epics with fewer than 3 features and no explicit
cross-feature handoff.

Use this table:

| ID | Producer feature | Consumer feature(s) | Shape | Payload | Style |
|----|------------------|---------------------|-------|---------|-------|

Rules:
- I-## is only for cross-feature interactions inside this epic. Use X-## for
  cross-epic integrations in docs/product/cross-epic-integration-map.md and
  E07-F01-cross-epic-map.md.
- I-## IDs are stable: assign once here and reference verbatim in
  decomposition, feature specs, producing/consuming task specs, feature review,
  task review, and QA.
- Each interaction's shape source resolves to a section in architecture.md.
  Producer and consumer features must point to the SAME architecture.md section.
- Producer feature and consumer feature(s) may be intended feature names at this
  phase; decomposition must create matching feature boundaries or update the map
  before advancing.
- Payload names the concrete data, event, file, state, API, CLI output, or
  artifact that crosses the feature boundary.
- Style names the interaction mode: API, event, shared data model, file
  artifact, CLI contract, UI handoff, or other concrete style.
- Register the document after writing it:
  `DOC_PATH="docs/plan/E07-F01/E07-F01-interaction-map.md" && shark related-docs add "Interaction Map" "${DOC_PATH}" --epic=E07-F01`

CRITICAL: Architecture must align with existing patterns from research report. Do not propose patterns that contradict CLAUDE.md conventions.

EXIT GATE:
- Architecture follows existing codebase patterns
- Every technical decision has rationale
- UAT scenarios map to epic success criteria
- No orphaned requirements
- E07-F01-cross-epic-map.md exists. If X-## rows are present, they match
  docs/product/cross-epic-integration-map.md, producer and consumer epic(s) are
  named, UX / CX handoff notes are reviewed or marked not applicable, and
  docs/product/progress.md has a decision-log entry for the update
- Multi-feature epic: E07-F01-interaction-map.md exists with at least one I-##
  row, and every row's shape source resolves to a section in architecture.md
